      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "The protective effect of breast-feeding against later obesity may be explained by the lower protein content compared with formula milk. However, the metabolic mechanisms remain unknown."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We studied the metabolic response to a higher or lower protein supply in infancy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN AND SETTING",
          "content": "The Childhood Obesity Project study is a double-blind, randomized, multicenter intervention trial. Infants were randomized to receive a higher (HP) or lower protein (LP) content infant formula or were breast-fed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Plasma samples of 691 infants who received formula milk with different protein content (HP, 2.05 g per 100 mL; LP, 1.25 g per 100 mL) or were breast-fed were collected."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Changes in plasma amino acid and acylcarnitine concentrations of 6-month-old infants according to different dietary protein supply were determined by liquid chromatography coupled to tandem mass spectrometry."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Twenty-nine metabolites differed significantly between the formula groups. Branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs) were the most discriminant metabolites. Their degradation products, the short-chain acylcarnitines C3, C4, and C5, were also significantly elevated in the HP group. A breakpoint analysis confirmed that with increasing BCAAs, the ratio between acylcarnitines and BCAAs decreases. Long-chain acylcarnitines were decreased in HP infants."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "BCAAs seem to play a pivotal role in the effect of a high-protein diet on β-oxidation and fat storage. We provide new evidence for a possible saturation of the BCAA degradation pathway that may represent the mechanism by which high-protein intake affects the metabolic regulation. Moreover, it appears to inhibit the initial step of the β-oxidation, thus leading to high early weight gain and body fat deposition."
        }
      ]},
